#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mandelbrot_set_member_q

#% text
# `mandelbrot_set_member_q(c)` gives `true` when the number `c` belongs to the Mandelbrot set, the set of `c` for which the orbit of 0 under `z -> z^2 + c` stays bounded.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The orbit is followed for `max_iterations` steps (default 1000) at machine precision, or at `working_precision` digits; `c` is a member when `abs(z) <= 2` throughout.

#% item
# Points in the main cardioid and in the disk of period 2 are recognised directly.

#% item
# Points very near the boundary need many iterations: a too small `max_iterations` makes them members.

#% item
# A list of numbers gives a list of answers.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mandelbrot_set_member_q(1 + im)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
mandelbrot_set_member_q([0, 1, im/2, im + 1])
#% output
#= [true, false, true, false]

#% cell
[mandelbrot_set_member_q(0.2501, max_iterations: 250), mandelbrot_set_member_q(0.2501, max_iterations: 500)]
#% output
#= [true, false]

#% cell
mandelbrot_set_member_q(im)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `julia_set_plot`, `mandelbrot_set_plot`
